Z||000008||tesmith66||12-20-2001||04:35 PM||||Aluminum would be a great choice if it weren't for all of its inherent problems. <P>For starters, the strongest aluminum is only about 1/3 as strong as the mildest steel, so you can't simply copy a steel frame in aluminum. You would have to use more in some areas and completely redesign it in other areas. The amount of material would increase, and the weight savings would not be as great as one would think. Simply building a frame from aluminum tubes won't work- as far as the Fiero space frame is concerned.<P>Secondly, it has much lower fatigue resistance. It won't take near the repeated flexing and shock loading that steel will. It also expands and contracts at a higher rate than steel, so a lot more slop would have to be designed into the hard mounting points for any of the steel components. Continued thermal cycling (heating up and cooling down) will weaken welds. The whole assembly would have to be riveted together.<P>Third, it would be very expensive. Structural aluminum is more expensive than steel.<P>The fourth problem is corrosion. Raw aluminum corrodes easily, and its structural properties are immediately affected. Steel can rust a lot before it weakens, but aluminum can't take much. Especially when it is stressed, as in a frame. Aluminum is hard to paint, and coatings such as aladyne or anodizing are too costly and difficult to apply to such a large object. Galvanic corrosion (being in contact with steel in the presence of salty or moist air) is also a BIG issue. This will weaken the material in critical areas such as under bolt heads. Not good.<P>All of that said, once all of those big problems (and several small ones) are worked out, I would guesstimate the frame would be 30% lighter and much stiffer. I beleive Pontiac had some working prototype aluminum frames, and I know Saab (or maybe Volvo) have some as well. It CAN be done, but it will be costly and time consuming. It would be easier if the body panels were integrated into the construction like the skins on an aircraft. That is what makes airplanes strong. The frame is comprised of many pieces held together by the skin.<P>Please don't think I'm bashing your idea, I think it's great and I encourage creativity such as this. I should probably also mention that I have been an aircraft structure designer for 13 years, and I have a little experience in this area. It's fun to be able to mix my career and Fiero hobby.<P>sorry for the long post.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||12-20-2001||06:08 PM||||i don't know how you would find out how they did it and/or designed it, but there was a road and track article (sometime in '88) where they were talking about i think just the cradle being made out of aluminum for a test vehicle (also using a quad4 engine so it was pretty light). just the cradle might be easier to pull off. <P>but don't count me as being interested. i don't think i could afford something like that and wouldn't want to personally encourage you and then not buy. i like the idea though.||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroSTETZ||12-20-2001||06:13 PM||||well, im not doing it just to sell- i wanna have a sub 2000bl fiero, dangit! the whole duster w/ v8 weighs in at less than a stock fiero- so an aluminum fiero... i was also thinking about (riveting?) the body panels to the frame- make all new panels from AL- might be ugly, but maybe metal partitions or something to add rigidity, with fiero panels over? the cradle should be an easy job, though- if we take this idea anywhere, i will post on it (still in the "hmmmmm") stage||||reg||1
Z||000011||artherd||12-21-2001||05:13 AM||||The 360's frame (and parts of the body) are all aluminum.  Some pannels are carbon.<P>It's an amazing car, and when they were done pouring $millions into development and testing, I think they saved three hundred pounds.  Total.<P>I'd say there are maybe five people on this fourm who would be wiling to cough up the dough nessicary for a developed Aluminum tubular frame.  Maybe three. I'm guessing that once these puppies were roadable (not just drag raceable, but truely roadable/roadraceable) price would be in the $10k range.<P>I probally wouldn't ever be one (never say never  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> because I am suspecting one would have to sacrafice a fair ammount of stock appearance to fit the tubes everywhere.<P>The clientel would for example already have rool cages weled into their cars today  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Aluminum is GREAT stuff, I use it whenever I can infact.  Beats the living crap out of steel.  (anodizing will likely be needed for corossion resistance BTW.) that or JetHot/ceramic coatings. Some of which *you* can paint on...   But it's got it's set of flaws, namely low stregenth, and easy faituite/stresscracks.<P>Frankly I'd be much more intrested in an all-carbon Tub chassis  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000012||tesmith66||12-21-2001||07:55 AM||||A tube chassis made of good old steel would be lighter, but it wouldn't have the energy absorbing crumple zones. The Fiero chassis is very strong. If you want to go under 2000 lbs, get rid of all the "non-essential" items like:<P>heater, A/C, lines and controls<BR>ducting<BR>gut the interior<BR>use an all aluminum v6<BR>aluminum radiator<BR>lexan windows<BR>light weight wheels<BR>light weight racing seats<BR>anything else the car doesn't need to be safe and legal<P>All of this may get you where you want to be, but then the car would be ugly, noisy and cold in the winter time.<P>And you wouldn't have any tunes.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000013||stsmithgt||12-21-2001||10:00 AM||||A buddy that buys vehicles for a buick\pontiac dealership brought by what looked like a small SUV that had rear independant susupension.  I can't remember what the name of it was.(duh..)  <BR>The housing of the suspension was all aluminum.  It was easy to see from the rear and thought polishing it up would look cool. <BR> I suppose if the car manufacturers are going aluminum, it could'nt be too bad of an idea if made strong enough to last.||||reg||1
